Summarize the main points of the text provided.: The Code of Hammurabi is a legal code that dates back to 1754 BC in ancient Mesopotamia. It was etched on a stele and placed in the temple of Marduk in Babylon, meant for all to see. The code, consisting of 282 laws, is considered one of the first examples of written law. Laws of different areas such as trade, property ownership, punishment for crimes, and family law are extensively covered in the code. Punishments for committing crimes are specified, making use of the 'an eye for an eye' mentality. Despite the apparent harshness, this type of punishment aimed to establish fairness and balance in society. The Code of Hammurabi set a vital foundation for future civilizations' legal systems, including Greek and Roman law, and it has been called the oldest legal framework known to humanity.The Code of Hammurabi is a set of 282 laws dating back to 1754 BC in ancient Mesopotamia. Displayed in the temple of Marduk, it extensively covers areas such as trade, property, crime, and family law. With its 'an eye for an eye' approach to punishment, the code aimed to establish fairness in society. Influencing future civilizations' legal systems, it is considered the oldest legal framework known.
