Identify three logical fallacies within the following argument and explain why they are fallacies.: Every year, controversial books are banned in schools, proving that classical books are the only ones worthy of study. Besides, some of the best-selling authors admit that they were heavily inspired by classics. Anyone who disagrees with this claim is disrespectful to centuries-old traditions and therefore cannot be an educated person.1. False dichotomy: The argument suggests that either classical books are the only ones worthy of study or someone is disrespecting centuries-old traditions. This fallacy implies there are only two options when, in reality, there may be other valid options or reasons to value contemporary literature.\n2. Appeal to authority: The author references best-selling authors who were inspired by classics, implying that their support validates the claim. This fallacy makes it seem as though the opinions of these authors are inherently superior to others, even though the evaluation of literature is subjective.\n3. Ad hominem: The argument implies that anyone who disagrees with the claim is disrespectful and uneducated. By attacking the character of the person and not their argument, this fallacy distracts from the real issue and doesn't address potential counterarguments based on merit.
