Using the information provided, create a suitable, hypothetical problem and then solve it.: Two cars are traveling in opposite directions on a straight road. Car A is traveling at 60 miles per hour, while car B is traveling at 40 miles per hour. The distance between the cars is currently 50 miles.Problem: How long will it take for the two cars to be 110 miles apart? \n\nSolution: Since the cars are moving in opposite directions, their relative speed is the sum of their individual speeds, which is 60 + 40 = 100 miles per hour. To find the time it takes for them to be 110 miles apart, we can use the formula 'distance = speed × time'. Rearranging the formula, we get 'time = distance ÷ speed', so the time will be 110 ÷ 100 = 1.1 hours.
