Explain the use of a hammerA hammer is a hand tool that is typically used for driving nails into wood or other materials, as well as for breaking objects apart, or for bending and shaping metal. The most commonly used hammer is called a claw hammer, which has a flat, large head on one side for striking, and a curved claw on the other for removing nails or prying things apart. To use a hammer, you hold the handle near the end for the most leverage, and then swing it to strike the target with the flat head. The weight of the hammer, combined with the motion of the swing, creates a powerful force that drives nails into the material or helps shape metal. The claw end of the hammer can also work as a lever to remove nails or pry something apart, increasing its versatility as a tool.
