Who are the current members of the United Nations Security Council?The United Nations Security Council is made up of 15 member countries, with five being permanent members and 10 being non-permanent members elected for two-year terms. The five permanent members of the Security Council are: China, France, the Russian Federation, the United Kingdom, and the United States. Each of these nations has the power to veto any substantive Security Council resolution.

As of 2021, the ten non-permanent members who have been elected for a two-year term are: Belgium, Dominican Republic, Estonia, Germany, Indonesia, Niger, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, South Africa, Tunisia, and Vietnam.

It's important to note that the non-permanent members of the Security Council change every two years, with five new members being elected each time.
