How does the human nervous system function?The nervous system is a complex network of cells and fibers that transmit information throughout the body. It is made up of the central nervous system and the peripheral nervous system. 

The central nervous system includes the brain and spinal cord, and functions as the body's control center. Information from the peripheral nervous system, which includes nerves that connect the brain and spinal cord to the rest of the body, is processed and interpreted by the brain. The brain then sends instructions to the body, directing movement and other actions. 

Neurons, or nerve cells, are the basic units of the nervous system. They transmit information via electrical and chemical signals. When a neuron is excited, an electrical charge travels along its length, culminating in the release of chemicals called neurotransmitters. These neurotransmitters cross a small gap, called a synapse, and bind to receptors on neighboring neurons, passing along the message. 

The nervous system is capable of responding rapidly to stimuli, allowing us to move and react quickly. It is also responsible for more subtle changes and long-term functions, such as regulating hormones and body temperature. Overall, the nervous system plays a critical role in coordinating and controlling the functions of the body.
Look up a famous figure and summarize their life and accomplishments.: Louis ArmstrongLouis Armstrong was an American jazz trumpeter, singer, and one of the most influential figures in jazz music. Born on August 4, 1901, in New Orleans, Armstrong grew up in poverty and began playing the cornet in a boy's home band. In the 1920s, he moved to Chicago and became a prominent figure in the city's jazz scene, playing in bands led by King Oliver and Fletcher Henderson. Later, he formed his own band and started making recordings, which brought him international fame. Armstrong's virtuosity on the trumpet and distinctively gravelly voice helped popularize jazz and make it accessible to wider audiences. Some of his most famous recordings include "What a Wonderful World," "Hello, Dolly!" and "Mack the Knife." In addition to his music career, Armstrong also appeared in films and on television, further cementing his status as a cultural icon. He continued performing until his death on July 6, 1971, leaving behind a legacy as one of the greatest musicians of all time.
