Provide three possible explanations for why a car would have trouble starting in cold weather.1. Thickened engine oil: Cold weather can cause engine oil to thicken, making it more resistant to flow. This can lead to increased friction within the engine, making it harder to start.\n2. Reduced battery capacity: Cold temperatures can reduce the efficiency of a car's battery. This may result in decreased voltage output, making it difficult for the battery to provide the necessary power to start the engine.\n3. Problems with fuel delivery: Cold weather may cause the fuel lines to freeze or the fuel itself to become less volatile. Either of these scenarios can hinder the fuel delivery system and make it difficult for the engine to start.
