Read the following passage and then explain which type of literary point of view is used (e.g., first person, second person, third person limited, or third person omniscient).: Lena sat at the kitchen table, sipping her tea and watching the raindrops slide down the windowpane. She thought about how she would go to the store when the rain stopped. She had never been fond of umbrellas, no matter how practical they were. Lena knew Jake would laugh at her stubbornness, always refusing to use an umbrella, but she didn't care. Unbeknownst to her, Jake felt the same way about umbrellas.The type of literary point of view used in this passage is third person limited. The reader has access to Lena's thoughts, feelings, and perspectives, but not to Jake's. The mention of Jake's feelings on umbrellas is only Lena's assumption, rather than knowing his thoughts directly, which would indicate third person omniscient.
