The equation y = -4.9t^2 + 23.8t describes the height (in meters) of a projectile launched from the ground at 23.8 meters per second. In how many seconds will the projectile first reach 28 meters in height?To find the time it takes for the projectile to reach a height of 28 meters, we can use the equation y = -4.9t^2 + 23.8t, where y is the height of the projectile in meters and t is the time in seconds. Substituting y = 28 into the equation, we get 28 = -4.9t^2 + 23.8t. Rearranging this equation, we obtain a quadratic equation of the form 7t^2 - 34t + 40 = 0, which can be factored as (7t - 20)(t - 2) = 0. Therefore, our possible values for t are 20/7 ≈ 2.857 or 2. Of these, we choose the smaller value of t, which is t = 2 seconds.
