Explain what a "convoluted neural network" is and how it differs from a regular neural network.A convoluted neural network (CNN) is a specific type of neural network often used in image and video recognition tasks. It differs from a regular neural network in that it is specifically designed to recognize patterns and features in a given input, by taking small, overlapping sections of the input and processing them in a way that identifies common features.

CNNs are made up of multiple layers, each of which is designed to identify a specific type of feature in the input. The first layer may be responsible for identifying edges, while the next may identify corners, and so on. These layers work together to determine the content of the input and make predictions or decisions based on what it sees.

One key advantage of CNNs over regular neural networks is that they are able to recognize patterns regardless of their positions in the input. So if the input is an image of a cat sitting on the left side of the frame, and the same cat sitting on the right side in another image, the CNN will be able to recognize it as a cat in both cases. This is achieved through the use of special techniques such as pooling, which reduces the size of the input while maintaining its overall composition.

Overall, the difference between a CNN and a regular neural network lies in the fact that CNNs are specifically designed to identify patterns and features in inputs, making them particularly useful for image and video recognition tasks.
Write Levenshtein distance in c++. Here is an example implementation of the Levenshtein distance algorithm in C++:

```
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int levenshtein_distance(string s1, string s2) {
    const int m = s1.size();
    const int n = s2.size();
    int dp[m + 1][n + 1];

    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
        dp[i][0] = i;
    }

    for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
        dp[0][j] = j;
    }

    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            dp[i][j] = min({dp[i - 1][j] + 1, dp[i][j - 1] + 1, dp[i - 1][j - 1] + (s1[i - 1] != s2[j - 1])});
        }
    }

    return dp[m][n];
}

int main() {
    string s1 = "kitten";
    string s2 = "sitting";
    cout << "Levenshtein distance between " << s1 << " and " << s2 << " is " << levenshtein_distance(s1, s2) << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This implementation calculates the Levenshtein distance between two input strings `s1` and `s2`. It first initializes a dynamic programming table `dp` with the base cases for empty strings, and then fills in the table by iterating through the strings and comparing each pair of characters. The final value in the table is the minimum Levenshtein distance between the two strings.
